{# The <head> lives in an included partial, and a Twig block inside an include
   cannot be overridden by a template that extends this one -- which is why
   every page used to be titled "Main Page". Declaring the block here puts it
   in the inheritance chain where pages can reach it; capturing it into a
   variable keeps its text out of the document body and means a page that
   declares no title still renders. #}
{% set page_title %}{% block title %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}

{# The same trick for the description, and for the same reason. Every page used
   to share one -- "Trip Builder – FlightHub PHP Coding Assessment" -- which on
   231 city pages is 231 pages claiming to be about the same thing. A page that
   declares none falls back to that, so nothing is left without one. #}
{% set page_description %}{% block description %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}

{# Whether this page draws a map, captured the same way and for the same
   reason: the stylesheet goes in the header, which is included before the
   content that would know.

   Worth the trouble because Mapbox GL is 503KB gzipped -- measured, not
   estimated -- and five page types out of eighteen draw a map. Loading it
   everywhere would put half a megabyte of JavaScript on the checkout.

   A page opts in with `{% block map %}yes{% endblock %}`; the marker itself is
   never printed. Both includes below inherit the context, so setting it here is
   enough for the header's stylesheet and the footer's script. #}
{% set page_map %}{% block map %}{% endblock %}{% endset %}
{% set needs_map = page_map|trim is not empty %}

{% include 'partials/header.html.twig' with {
    'page_title': page_title|trim,
    'page_description': page_description|trim
} %}
{% include 'partials/breadcrumbs.html.twig' %}

{# Everything between the header and the footer, in one box.

   The box is what a sticky child is measured against. Without it the page's
   bands are siblings of the footer inside <body>, so the search bar's
   containing block ran to the bottom of the document and the bar stayed pinned
   over the footer -- still offering to search from on top of the copyright.
   Bounded here, it is carried up and out of the way as the footer arrives,
   which is what a sticky element does when it is given an end.

   The alternative was to watch the footer from a scroll handler and unpin on
   the way past. A scroll runs on the compositor and a scroll handler does not,
   so that is a frame late every time -- the same jump the hero tray had before
   it was rebuilt on `position: sticky`. #}
<div class="page">
    {% block content %}{{ page_content|default('')|raw }}{% endblock %}
</div>

{% if not consent.answered %}
    {% include 'partials/cookie-notice.html.twig' %}
{% endif %}

{% include 'partials/footer.html.twig' %}
